WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.268 --> 00:00:04.470
Welcome to the Forge.
Look down at your anvils,

00:00:04.503 --> 00:00:06.372
you'll notice
that there's nothing there.

00:00:06.472 --> 00:00:08.107
That's because
there's a little twist.

00:00:08.207 --> 00:00:10.643
You'll be making
signature blades

00:00:10.743 --> 00:00:12.412
using this.

00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:16.315
[laughing]

00:00:16.482 --> 00:00:18.584
<i> A massive chunk</i>

00:00:18.751 --> 00:00:21.287
<i> of twisted elevator cable.</i>

00:00:21.353 --> 00:00:24.457
I-I've worked
on cable knives before,

00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:27.359
but nothing of that size.

00:00:27.426 --> 00:00:30.663
Your three-hour forge time
starts... now.

00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:33.366
My plan is to knock
all the crud out of the cable

00:00:33.466 --> 00:00:36.969
on the inside of it
so that I can get a clean weld.

00:00:37.069 --> 00:00:38.471
<i> Get the grease out,</i>

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:40.173
get the flux in.

00:00:42.175 --> 00:00:43.008
Is that a pyrometer?

00:00:43.142 --> 00:00:45.244 line:0%
Paul, is that a pyrometer?

00:00:45.344 --> 00:00:48.081 line:0%
Yes, sir. I like to know
what temperature my steel is

00:00:48.181 --> 00:00:49.449 line:0%
before I quench it.

00:00:50.616 --> 00:00:52.084 line:0%
Wow! That's smart.

00:00:52.217 --> 00:00:53.986
A lot of guys judge
temperatures by color,

00:00:54.086 --> 00:00:55.488
but when you're forge-welding,

00:00:55.588 --> 00:00:57.657
<i> I really like to know exactly
 what the temperature is.</i>

00:00:57.757 --> 00:01:00.126
Wow, J., I've never seen you
have a total man-crush

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:01.761
on somebody's answer.

00:01:01.861 --> 00:01:03.529
[laughing]

00:01:03.663 --> 00:01:05.532
Well, actually,
I'm about to break that crush.

00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:08.233
I applaud Paul
for bringing the pyrometer,

00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:09.802
but it also is a sign
of somebody

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:11.303
who's probably
really meticulous,

00:01:11.437 --> 00:01:14.674
and I'm watching Paul trying
to scrub this cable clean,

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.075
<i> and that's time wasted.</i>

00:01:16.142 --> 00:01:18.310
It's easier to just heat it up
a little bit,

00:01:18.377 --> 00:01:21.180
tap it slightly open
to get all that heat in there,

00:01:21.281 --> 00:01:23.883
cook all of that out,
and then twist it closed.

00:01:24.484 --> 00:01:27.152
Stuff is dirty.
It's gonna weld.

00:01:27.286 --> 00:01:29.789
The hardest part
about using cable to make steel

00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:31.524
is making sure
that welds will stick,

00:01:31.657 --> 00:01:33.659
and I'm afraid
it's just not clean enough.

00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:36.161
Oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh!

00:01:36.261 --> 00:01:39.232
-Oh!
-Oh my goodness, man!

00:01:39.699 --> 00:01:40.900
That didn't work.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.702
It just falls all apart.

00:01:42.836 --> 00:01:45.438
I should've waited
to work on this.

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:48.108
To twist this cable,
you have to get it up to heat.

00:01:50.342 --> 00:01:52.745
-Oh, hell.
-Yeah. This is done.

00:01:53.712 --> 00:01:56.749
<i> I can grab another piece
 of cable and start again,</i>

00:01:56.816 --> 00:01:59.853
but I don't... I don't like
the cable to begin with

00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:01.921
in terms of its cleanliness,
you know?

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:04.356
I just decide
it's gonna be a canister.

00:02:04.457 --> 00:02:07.559 line:0%
It's a workaround,
but is it a legit workaround?

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:10.429 line:0%
We didn't say they needed
to make cable Damascus.

00:02:10.562 --> 00:02:13.365 line:0%
We said they needed to work
with that cable.

00:02:13.532 --> 00:02:15.434
Canister Damascus is something
I've never done before.

00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:18.738
<i> But I just feel like
 the process is more foolproof</i>

00:02:18.804 --> 00:02:22.375
than taking a risk on the cable
just not welding well.

00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:25.177
Something that he's never done
before. That's a ballsy move.

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:27.046
[Wil] Steven doesn't have
the time

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:29.148
to make a mistake
with a canister.

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:32.519
That is a lot of pressure.

00:02:32.619 --> 00:02:33.853
That's my new plan.

00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:35.721
Let's just look over here
at Robert.

00:02:35.888 --> 00:02:38.524
He's got a huge pipe wrench

00:02:38.624 --> 00:02:40.626
on that cable,
and he's twisting it up.

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:42.495
That's what we expect to see,
is it not?

00:02:42.628 --> 00:02:44.396
Right, and if you do
the cable properly,

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.700
as you twist it, it'll actually
start forge-welding.

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:49.235
Wow.

00:02:49.903 --> 00:02:52.838
[J.] And see, Paul,
everything's staying tight.

00:02:52.938 --> 00:02:54.540
He put a lot of weld on the end.

00:02:54.573 --> 00:02:58.178
See, there's the difference
between Paul and Ryan there.

00:02:59.712 --> 00:03:02.515
I might have to fold this.
It's not wide enough.

00:03:03.015 --> 00:03:07.353
It's getting a little longer
and leaner than I really want.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.255
<i> I have to fold it
 and get it thick enough</i>

00:03:10.322 --> 00:03:12.458
so that I'd have something
to work with.

00:03:14.761 --> 00:03:16.728
[all exclaiming]

00:03:16.762 --> 00:03:17.497
[laughing]

00:03:17.664 --> 00:03:19.198
[Robert]
Who designed that hammer?

00:03:19.332 --> 00:03:21.467
That's a guy
who hits metal hard.

00:03:23.336 --> 00:03:24.671
[J.] What the heck
is Steven doin'?

00:03:24.837 --> 00:03:26.839
He's standing there
watching everybody else.

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:28.874
[Steven]<i> I give the canister
 30 minutes in the forge</i>

00:03:28.974 --> 00:03:32.511
because I want that thing to be
hot enough to set the weld.

00:03:32.912 --> 00:03:35.949
So for that 30 minutes,
I'm standing here sweatin'.

00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:37.850
It's not going to weld
itself together.

00:03:37.916 --> 00:03:41.654
I'm standing here stringing
curse words together to myself.

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:43.756
Steven! You all right?

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:45.391
Yeah, I'm just waitin' for heat.

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:47.026
-Just making sure.
-Yeah.

00:03:47.192 --> 00:03:49.429
I... I'm not looking
very smart right now.

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:51.197
-It's gotta be hot, right?
-Got to be hot.

00:03:51.664 --> 00:03:53.298
Here we go.
Steven's on the move.

00:03:53.432 --> 00:03:56.769
I can't really see
if it's hot enough or not.

00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:01.173
Well, that went...
There went that.

00:04:01.273 --> 00:04:02.908
-I hope it's nothing serious.
-No!

00:04:02.975 --> 00:04:04.543
Not welded. [bleep]

00:04:04.643 --> 00:04:07.313
Steven's shaking his head,
but, like, it looks pretty good.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:10.049
-Yeah, I'd keep going.
-He's got plenty of heat.

00:04:11.184 --> 00:04:14.921
He's gotta be fast. He's gotta
catch up to these other smiths.

00:04:17.723 --> 00:04:21.261
I'm not out of the woods yet.
Still need to harden this blade.

00:04:23.362 --> 00:04:25.297
[Robert]<i> I'm pleased
 with the shape,</i>

00:04:25.397 --> 00:04:28.001
and I've gotta get
this thing in the oil.

00:04:30.168 --> 00:04:32.337
Pull it out, and I got a warp.

00:04:32.471 --> 00:04:35.408
Fortunately, it's still warm.
I can move it.

00:04:35.474 --> 00:04:37.210
You don't very often see
a smith smacking

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:39.011
on a blade with a hammer
right after he quenched it.

00:04:39.111 --> 00:04:41.614
Because there's a chance of
shattering the blade, correct?

00:04:43.416 --> 00:04:44.717
Paul, getting ready.

00:04:44.784 --> 00:04:47.420
[intense music playing]

00:04:52.124 --> 00:04:56.296
Blade's hard, I'm like, "Yeah,
that's what I'm here to do."

00:04:58.097 --> 00:05:00.099
[Doug] Steve just quenched
his blade.

00:05:00.166 --> 00:05:02.835
Steven has made up
a lot of ground here.

00:05:04.003 --> 00:05:06.706
Three, two, one!

00:05:09.075 --> 00:05:10.876
Bladesmiths,
shut down your machines

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:12.645
'cause this round is over!

00:05:12.678 --> 00:05:15.013
Bladesmiths, welcome
to our strength test,

00:05:15.047 --> 00:05:16.682
the wire cable chop.

00:05:17.283 --> 00:05:19.352
I'm going to take
each of your blades,

00:05:19.519 --> 00:05:21.587
I'm gonna baton them
three times

00:05:21.621 --> 00:05:23.455
at three different points.

00:05:23.555 --> 00:05:26.492
Keep in mind, it's not what
your blade can do to this cable.

00:05:26.659 --> 00:05:28.661
It's what the cable's gonna do
to your blades.

00:05:29.228 --> 00:05:30.563
Steven, what do you think?

00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:33.466
I think we might be in trouble.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.268 line:0%
<i> When I see the cable wire,</i>

00:05:36.402 --> 00:05:38.471 line:0%
<i> I'm even more worried
 than I was before</i>

00:05:38.537 --> 00:05:40.039 line:0%
<i> because it's so thick.</i>

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:43.009
There's a good chance that
my blade will just snap in half.

00:05:45.177 --> 00:05:48.081
[hard rock music playing]

00:05:50.917 --> 00:05:52.885
[music fades]

00:05:53.052 --> 00:05:54.754
[laughs]

00:05:56.722 --> 00:05:58.757
[J.] Well, Steven,
it held up pretty well.

00:05:59.224 --> 00:06:00.592
You do have some wrinkling,

00:06:00.692 --> 00:06:02.461
especially
on the first two strikes.

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:04.363
Third one, not as much.

00:06:04.396 --> 00:06:06.599
But the handle's nice and tight.

00:06:06.666 --> 00:06:08.701
Nice job.

00:06:08.834 --> 00:06:11.203
So you saw that, Paul.
What are you thinkin'? Ready?

00:06:11.269 --> 00:06:13.606
Uh, I'm as ready as I can be.

00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:16.709 line:0%
[suspenseful music playing]

00:06:19.144 --> 00:06:20.780
[intense music playing]

00:06:23.416 --> 00:06:25.051
[music fades]

00:06:26.285 --> 00:06:28.521
[tense music playing]

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:31.023
[J.] Well, Paul,

00:06:31.056 --> 00:06:33.559
you definitely have some
chipping out on the edge here,

00:06:33.593 --> 00:06:36.796
especially
on the last two strikes.

00:06:36.896 --> 00:06:39.031
But stayed in alignment.

00:06:39.131 --> 00:06:41.567
Everything's tight. Nice job.

00:06:42.167 --> 00:06:43.503
What are you thinkin', Robert?

00:06:43.536 --> 00:06:46.939
The steam hammer in my chest
says, "Let's get her done."

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:51.510 line:0%
<i> Well, that's it.
 It's gonna break.</i>

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:54.313 line:0%
<i> There's no way he can put
 that weak spot</i>

00:06:54.379 --> 00:06:56.682 line:0%
on hardened cable

00:06:56.783 --> 00:06:57.950
and it'll stay.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:00.553
[intense music playing]

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:06.258
[music fades]

00:07:06.425 --> 00:07:08.561
[suspenseful music playing]

00:07:10.195 --> 00:07:12.064
[J.] Well, Robert, it survived.

00:07:12.898 --> 00:07:15.000
But that crack at the edge
has opened up now.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:17.503
There's definitely
separation damage

00:07:17.603 --> 00:07:19.172
from the initial break.

00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:22.842
Definitely had a chipping out
on the three spots I struck.

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:24.844
That being said,

00:07:24.944 --> 00:07:27.045
you made the best
of a bad situation.

00:07:27.079 --> 00:07:29.081
-Good job.
-Thank you.

00:07:29.214 --> 00:07:30.516
Doug?

00:07:31.717 --> 00:07:33.853
Bladesmiths, this is
the sharpness test.

00:07:34.586 --> 00:07:36.121
To see how sharp
your blade is,

00:07:36.221 --> 00:07:37.256
I will take your weapon

00:07:37.389 --> 00:07:39.325
and attempt to cut
through this fire hose.

00:07:39.725 --> 00:07:42.161
-Steven, you're up first. Ready?
-Yes, sir.

00:07:42.595 --> 00:07:45.131
<i> In round one,
 the judges didn't really like</i>

00:07:45.231 --> 00:07:47.532
the little whoopty thing
in the edge.

00:07:47.666 --> 00:07:49.468
<i> I was able to make
 a lot of improvement,</i>

00:07:49.501 --> 00:07:51.303
<i> but there's always a chance</i>

00:07:51.403 --> 00:07:53.072
<i> that something could go wrong.</i>

00:07:53.172 --> 00:07:55.441
[intense music playing]

00:07:55.575 --> 00:07:57.143
[music fades]

00:07:59.078 --> 00:08:00.246
Wow.

00:08:00.346 --> 00:08:02.314
Okay, Steven.
First of all, your handle.

00:08:02.447 --> 00:08:03.849
It is wide.

00:08:03.882 --> 00:08:07.219
Because of its wideness,
it keeps wanting to turn over.

00:08:07.619 --> 00:08:10.188
But barring that,
your edge is very sharp.

00:08:10.222 --> 00:08:11.991
Your blade will cut.

00:08:12.124 --> 00:08:13.359
Thanks.

00:08:13.492 --> 00:08:15.294
[Doug] Paul, you're up next.

00:08:15.394 --> 00:08:18.030
[intense music playing]

00:08:27.239 --> 00:08:28.273
Okay, Paul.

00:08:28.307 --> 00:08:30.643
This feels very good
in the hand.

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:33.879
Not only do I feel like
I can cut forward,

00:08:33.912 --> 00:08:36.482
but immediately I'm ready
to do follow-up thrusting

00:08:36.615 --> 00:08:38.183
or another slash.

00:08:38.283 --> 00:08:40.819
Overall, your blade will cut.

00:08:40.853 --> 00:08:42.955
-Thank you. Thank you.
-Good job.

00:08:43.022 --> 00:08:45.124
Robert, you're up next.

00:08:45.624 --> 00:08:47.393
[Robert]<i> Since that crack
 opened up a bit,</i>

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:51.130
<i> I'm really worried
 about the blade catching</i>

00:08:51.263 --> 00:08:53.065
and just halting mid-stroke.

00:08:53.165 --> 00:08:55.201
[dramatic music playing]

00:08:59.939 --> 00:09:01.874
Okay, Robert.
Your design here

00:09:01.974 --> 00:09:05.644
is a nice, clean arc
that allows for a good slice.

00:09:05.744 --> 00:09:08.213
You definitely gave us
a chopper here.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:10.382
Your weapon, sir, will cut.

00:09:10.482 --> 00:09:11.616
Thank you.

00:09:11.716 --> 00:09:13.351
[Wil] Bladesmiths, the judges

00:09:13.451 --> 00:09:15.120
have made their final decision,

00:09:15.221 --> 00:09:18.157
and it's time for one of you
to leave the Forge.

00:09:18.257 --> 00:09:20.893
[dramatic music playing]

00:09:22.561 --> 00:09:24.996
Robert, your blade
did not make the cut.

00:09:25.831 --> 00:09:28.267
Robert, you did something
we've never had happen

00:09:28.334 --> 00:09:29.835
on this competition.
You came back

00:09:29.868 --> 00:09:32.371 line:0%
from a catastrophic
blade failure,

00:09:32.505 --> 00:09:34.439 line:0%
<i> found a workaround,
 and actually made it</i>

00:09:34.573 --> 00:09:36.041 line:0%
<i> into the testing
 of that weapon.</i>

00:09:36.541 --> 00:09:38.543
But in the end,
your blade took more damage

00:09:38.677 --> 00:09:40.479
than the other blades,
and we've gotta let you go.

00:09:40.579 --> 00:09:41.781
Understand.

00:09:41.881 --> 00:09:44.149
Robert, pleasure surrender
your blade.

00:09:44.649 --> 00:09:46.652
[Robert]<i> I would love
 to move on,</i>

00:09:46.752 --> 00:09:50.589
<i> but my best lessons
 have been through failure.</i>

00:09:50.722 --> 00:09:53.091
So just being here is a win.

00:09:53.158 --> 00:09:56.629
<i>And my family, they'll be happy
 I didn't give up.</i>

00:09:56.729 --> 00:09:59.865
They'll just revert back to,
"Oh, yeah, that's MacGyver.

00:10:00.365 --> 00:10:02.001
<i> He'll figure something out."</i>

